WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:04.340
This video follows a call from start to finish.

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:06.104
First an inbound call:

00:00:06.104 --> 00:00:11.650
the recording notice, the welcome, the menu, the queue, the offer and the wrap-up.

00:00:11.650 --> 00:00:14.990
Then an outbound call and the checks it passes.

00:00:14.990 --> 00:00:16.376
Then a callback.

00:00:16.376 --> 00:00:19.968
For each stage you see the screen that controls it.

00:00:19.968 --> 00:00:24.379
Calls never connect on this demo site, so what happens in a live

00:00:24.379 --> 00:00:26.270
call is shown in slides.

00:00:30.870 --> 00:00:34.553
A call to your number reaches an inbound entry point.

00:00:34.553 --> 00:00:39.547
If recording is announced to inbound callers, that notice is spoken first.

00:00:39.547 --> 00:00:43.730
Then the welcome message plays, and the menu if there is one.

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:46.748
Then the caller joins the queue.

00:00:46.748 --> 00:00:50.209
If the queue is full, the queue-full action applies.

00:00:50.209 --> 00:00:55.103
While the caller waits, the queue's treatment plays, and an agent is offered

00:00:55.103 --> 00:00:57.610
the call as soon as one is eligible.

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:02.927
The agent accepts, talks, and can hold, transfer or conference.

00:01:02.927 --> 00:01:07.626
When the call ends, the agent is in wrap-up until the activity is

00:01:07.626 --> 00:01:10.737
completed, or until fifteen minutes pass.

00:01:10.737 --> 00:01:13.980
The disposition then runs the subject flow.

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:21.202
On the entry point, the Welcome and I.V.R.

00:01:21.202 --> 00:01:23.759
menu card holds the Welcome message.

00:01:23.759 --> 00:01:28.325
It is spoken after the recording notice, and skipped when it is empty

00:01:28.325 --> 00:01:30.090
and there is no notice.

00:01:30.578 --> 00:01:31.958
Build an I.V.R.

00:01:31.958 --> 00:01:33.470
menu adds a menu.

00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:37.743
Tries is how many times a caller may press a wrong key, and

00:01:37.743 --> 00:01:41.162
When the tries run out says what happens next.

00:01:41.162 --> 00:01:46.092
Each key sends the caller to a queue, an agent, a submenu, voicemail,

00:01:46.092 --> 00:01:49.708
an approved external number, or repeats the menu.

00:01:53.922 --> 00:01:57.541
On the queue, While callers wait sets the treatment.

00:01:57.541 --> 00:01:59.850
The Welcome message plays once.

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:01.847
Hold music loops after it.

00:02:01.847 --> 00:02:05.466
With no music selected, callers hear a ringing tone.

00:02:05.466 --> 00:02:10.022
A caller who is offered to an agent at once hears no announcements.

00:02:10.499 --> 00:02:16.495
Announce the caller's place in line says you are number N, at every Announcement interval.

00:02:16.495 --> 00:02:21.805
Announce the estimated wait speaks only when an agent is available, and Average

00:02:21.805 --> 00:02:23.804
handle time is above zero.

00:02:23.804 --> 00:02:28.239
Minimum and maximum announced estimates keep the figure in range.

00:02:28.693 --> 00:02:32.842
The Callback card offers a callback key after a number of seconds.

00:02:32.842 --> 00:02:36.990
A caller who presses it leaves the queue and is called back later.

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:40.333
It needs the Contact Center Outbound Dialer feature.

00:02:44.582 --> 00:02:51.727
Limits holds Maximum callers waiting with its Queue-full action, and Maximum wait with its Maximum-wait action.

00:02:51.727 --> 00:02:56.125
Overflow moves the caller to the next queue they have not visited.

00:02:56.125 --> 00:02:59.362
Send to voicemail ends the wait with a message.

00:02:59.886 --> 00:03:05.802
Voicemail can be delivered to an agent's inbox, or to the queue's shared voicemail box.

00:03:05.802 --> 00:03:10.826
Interaction Center, Shared voicemail is where the shared box is reviewed.

00:03:15.126 --> 00:03:18.027
Every call ends with a disposition.

00:03:18.027 --> 00:03:22.837
Subject Flows, Manage Flow decides what each disposition does:

00:03:22.837 --> 00:03:27.506
finish, try again, create a new activity, or convert a lead.

00:03:28.065 --> 00:03:31.286
Agent states holds the reasons agents choose.

00:03:31.286 --> 00:03:36.275
After a call, the agent stays in wrap-up until the activity is completed.

00:03:40.475 --> 00:03:45.748
For an outbound call, the dialer checks the record before it dials, in this order.

00:03:45.748 --> 00:03:47.305
The number is valid.

00:03:47.305 --> 00:03:49.881
It is not known to be out of service.

00:03:49.881 --> 00:03:53.895
The record has attempts left, and the retry delay has passed.

00:03:54.645 --> 00:03:58.933
Then the contact has not opted out, the calling window is open, the

00:03:58.933 --> 00:04:04.514
abandonment cap allows dialing, and the number is not on a national do-not-call registry.

00:04:04.514 --> 00:04:08.155
A record that fails is skipped or cancelled, not dialed.

00:04:08.905 --> 00:04:15.002
A call that ends before an agent is connected is dispositioned by the dialer itself:

00:04:15.002 --> 00:04:21.709
no answer, busy, answering machine, rejected, failed, disconnected, or number not in service.

00:04:21.709 --> 00:04:24.215
Only some of those are retried.

00:04:25.857 --> 00:04:28.312
The rules live on the dialer profile.

00:04:28.312 --> 00:04:32.881
The Dialing card has Max attempts and Retry delay, and the Compliance card

00:04:32.881 --> 00:04:36.135
has the do-not-call setting and the calling window.

00:04:36.135 --> 00:04:39.847
The subject flow decides first, and these are the fallback.

00:04:44.047 --> 00:04:48.169
A callback starts when a caller presses the callback key in the queue.

00:04:48.169 --> 00:04:52.346
The caller is removed from the queue, and a background task creates the

00:04:52.346 --> 00:04:54.027
callback within a minute.

00:04:54.777 --> 00:04:59.436
The callback is queued again at the queue's default priority, and an agent

00:04:59.436 --> 00:05:01.765
is offered it like a preview call.

00:05:01.765 --> 00:05:05.317
The agent accepts, and the platform dials the customer.

00:05:06.067 --> 00:05:11.071
Callbacks use a built-in preview profile that skips the do-not-call and calling window

00:05:11.071 --> 00:05:14.007
checks, because the customer asked to be called.

00:05:14.007 --> 00:05:19.337
The attempt limit, the cool-down between attempts, and the not-in-service check still apply.

00:05:23.537 --> 00:05:26.043
Check your flow from the top:

00:05:26.043 --> 00:05:32.487
notice, welcome, menu, queue, then the limits that protect callers from a long wait.

00:05:33.237 --> 00:05:37.007
Use callbacks to cut the abandon rate on a busy queue.

00:05:37.757 --> 00:05:42.408
Review the dispositions of the subject flow, so every outcome of a call

00:05:42.408 --> 00:05:43.737
has a next step.

00:05:45.737 --> 00:05:51.397
An inbound call passes the notice, the welcome, the menu and the queue before an agent.

00:05:51.397 --> 00:05:54.866
An outbound call passes the checks before it dials.

00:05:54.866 --> 00:05:58.335
A callback is a queued call the customer asked for.

00:05:58.335 --> 00:05:59.917
Thanks for watching.

